'''''[[Strictly Come Dancing]]''''' returned for its '''seventh series''' on 18 September 2009 on [[BBC One]]. [[Bruce Forsyth]] and [[Tess Daly]] returned as presenters of the main show on BBC One, while [[Claudia Winkleman]] presented the spin-off show ''[[Strictly Come Dancing: It Takes Two]]'' on [[BBC Two]]. [[Len Goodman]], [[Craig Revel Horwood]], and [[Bruno Tonioli]] returned as judges. [[Strictly Come Dancing series 5|Series 5]] winner [[Alesha Dixon]] joined the judging panel as a replacement for [[Arlene Phillips]].<ref name="Dixon joins Strictly dance judges">{{cite news |title=Dixon joins Strictly dance judges|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/entertainment/8142139.stm |work=BBC News|date=9 July 2009|accessdate=26 August 2009}}</ref> [[Darcey Bussell]], who later became a permanent judge from 2012 to 2018, served as a guest judge beginning with the quarterfinal.<ref name="Dixon joins Strictly dance judges" />

[[Camilla Dallerup]], [[Karen Hardy]], and [[Hayley Holt]] were replaced as professional female dancers by [[Natalie Lowe]], [[Aliona Vilani]], and [[Katya Virshilas]].<ref name="Strictly reveals dancing line-up ">{{cite news |title=Strictly reveals dancing line-up judges|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/entertainment/8220020.stm |work=BBC News|date=25 August 2009|accessdate=26 August 2009}}</ref> The show on 7 November was filmed from the [[Blackpool Tower Ballroom]] for the first time since the finale of [[Strictly Come Dancing series 2|series 2]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Alex |last=Fletcher |title=BBC holds 'Strictly' show in Blackpool|url=http://www.digitalspy.co.uk/strictlycomedancing/a176449/bbc-holds-strictly-show-in-blackpool.html |work= [[Digital Spy]]|date=8 September 2009|accessdate=22 October 2009}}</ref>

Due to illness, Bruce Forsyth did not appear on the show on 14 November. Tess Daly served as main presenter for the episode alongside the "surprise" addition of [[Ronnie Corbett]],<ref>{{cite web|title=Ronnie Corbett steps in for Bruce Forsyth on Strictly Come Dancing|first=Laura|last=Donnelly|work=[[The Daily Telegraph|The Telegraph]]|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/culture/tvandradio/strictly-come-dancing/6570684/Ronnie-Corbett-steps-in-for-Bruce-Forsyth-on-Strictly-Come-Dancing.html|date=14 November 2009|access-date=9 November 2025}}</ref> while Claudia Winkleman took over Daly's normal role. Daly and Winkleman both later held these positions permanently from 2014 to 2025.

''[[BBC Breakfast]]'' sports presenter [[Chris Hollins (broadcaster)|Chris Hollins]] and [[Ola Jordan]] were announced as the winners on 19 December, while ''[[Hollyoaks]]'' actor [[Ricky Whittle]] and [[Natalie Lowe]] finished in second place.

