== Reception ==
In September 2009 ''Strictly Come Dancing'' earned a place in the [[Guinness World Records]] as the "most successful reality television format",<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.broadcastnow.co.uk/news/international/strictly-come-dancing-is-worlds-biggest-format/5005691.article|accessdate=20 December 2009|title=Strictly Come Dancing is world's biggest format|work=[[Broadcast (magazine)|Broadcast]]|date=15 September 2009}}</ref> with licensing rights sold to broadcasters in 38 countries.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/culture/tvandradio/6189595/Strictly-Come-Dancing-is-the-worlds-most-successful-reality-format.html|accessdate=20 December 2009|title=Strictly Come Dancing is the world's most successful reality format|work=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=15 September 2009|first=Anita|last=Singh }}</ref> Nonetheless, the [[Strictly Come Dancing (series 6)|sixth series']] audiences had suffered as a result of direct clashes with [[ITV (TV network)|ITV]] rival ''[[The X Factor (UK series 5)|The X Factor]]''.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/newstopics/celebritynews/3570195/Beyonce-Knowles-to-perform-on-the-X-Factor-final.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081210090504/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/newstopics/celebritynews/3570195/Beyonce-Knowles-to-perform-on-the-X-Factor-final.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=10 December 2008|title=Beyonce Knowles to perform on the X Factor final|work=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=6 December 2009|first=Urmee|last=Khan|accessdate=20 December 2009 }}</ref> The BBC's decision in August 2009 to screen the seventh series on Saturday nights only was partly an attempt to reduce conflict with the rival talent show, as ITV had hinted that ''The X Factor''<nowiki/>'s results show would be broadcast on Sunday evening.<ref name=telegraph-saturdaymove>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/culture/tvandradio/strictly-come-dancing/6014913/Strictly-Come-Dancing-to-screen-on-Saturday-only.html|title=Strictly Come Dancing to screen on Saturday only|date=12 August 2009|accessdate=20 December 2009|work=The Daily Telegraph |location=London }}</ref> Sources at the BBC described the move as "better for the viewers... people at home lose out if things are competing against one another... We wanted to make ''Strictly Come Dancing'' an unmissable TV event".<ref name=telegraph-saturdaymove/>

However, the BBC still came under intense criticism when the extended Saturday show, initially running from 7–9pm, clashed almost entirely with ''The X Factor''<nowiki/>'s main show.<ref name=guardian-saturdaymove>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2009/sep/09/bbc-strictly-come-dancing-x-factor|accessdate=21 December 2009|title=BBC schedules Strictly Come Dancing head to head with The X Factor|date=9 September 2009|first=Mark|last=Sweny|work=The Guardian |location=London }}</ref> ITV sources accused the BBC of "effectively splitting the audience".<ref name=guardian-saturdaymove/> Writing in ''[[The Guardian]]'' George Dixon, head of scheduling for BBC One, argued that "the ability of viewers to see programmes again, whether via personal video recorders like [[Sky+]], online catch-up services including [[iPlayer]] or on channels such as [[E4 (channel)|E4]] or [[ITV2]], [makes] the notion of 'forcing' viewers to watch... outdated", adding that "There are around seven repeats of ''The X Factor'' on ITV1 and ITV2 each week".<ref name=guardian-scheduling>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/organgrinder/2009/sep/22/strictly-come-dancing-bbc1-scheduler|title=Strictly Come Dancing is just one small part of the schedulers' ballet|date=22 September 2009|accessdate=21 December 2009|first=George|last=Dixon|work=The Guardian |location=London }}</ref>

The scheduling conflict ended with ''The X Factor'' securing a higher audience throughout the series. On 22 October 2009, the BBC rescheduled the Saturday show, now down to eight contestants, so that the overlap between the two programs was only 45 minutes.<ref name=sky-reschedule>{{cite news|url=http://news.sky.com/skynews/Home/Showbiz-News/Strictly-Come-Dancing-Is-Moved-To-Earlier-Time-Slot-But-BBC-Denies-Giving-Way-To-X-Factor/Article/200910415411555?f=rss|title=Strictly Come Dancing is moved to earlier time slot but BBC denies giving way to X Factor|publisher=[[Sky News]]|first=Hannah|last=Stott|date=22 October 2009|accessdate=21 December 2009}}</ref> The BBC denied that the move was in direct response to the success of its ITV rival, saying instead that the move was to accommodate a new series with impressionist Jon Culshaw.<ref name=sky-reschedule/> ''Strictly Come Dancing''<nowiki/>'s runtime, previously fixed at two hours, was also expected to decrease as contestants were progressively eliminated, leading to a further reduction in overlap.

